  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to peace consolidation in Colombia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Colombian government has declared a policy of &#xbf;Total Peace&#xbf; as a key political priority, with the aim to end conflicts which have led to alarming levels of killings and intimidation, in particular of local leaders, human rights defenders and ex-combatants of the Revolutionary Armed Forces of Colombia &#xbf; People&apos;s Army (FARC). The policy relies simultaneously on dialogue with all groups rather than the use of military force, predominantly privileged in the past. The &#xbf;Total Peace&#xbf; agenda opens the perspectives for ending decades of internal conflict, but it is also extremely complex and needs fast and strong support from the international community. There is a strong vision and ambition from the Government, but at the same time a lack of resources, capacity, organisation and structure. This 18-month measure therefore aims contribute to the Colombian Government&apos;s peace efforts by supporting the Office of the High Commissioner for Peace, other peace-related institutions and relevant stakeholders, in order to support and advance peace building efforts in the country, with a focus on the Pacific and Amazon regions, which are of crucial importance for the sustainability of peace. It will focus on the Government&#xbf;s peace initiatives towards criminal gangs in the main urban centres and armed groups in the rural vicinities, foster dialogue within the communities and between communities and state institutions, and seek to create conditions for public and private investment and complement, accompany and achieve advances in the peace process between the Government and the &#xbf;Estado Mayor Central&#xbf; (EMC), with a strong focus on inclusion.</narrative>
  </description>
